To the team at DataKind,

#v(1fr)

// Explain my background leading to my passion in social good and data science

When I was in high school I had the opportunity to work for a conservation organization that decided my career trajectory. From a young age I had an interest in computers and data, but had never connected it to being able to do good in the world. This experience, and the idea that digital information can do good, inspired all of my work. And is the reason I am so well aligned with your goals.

// Explain why I fit so well for the job with datakind

Over the past 5 years I have worked for a number of conservation, medical and development non-profits to build essential data science and machine learning tools. My expertize ranges through data science, machine learning, cloud infrastructure, software development, and strong collaboration.

Most recently I founded and built a online platform for bioacoustic analysis tailored to let non-technical staff rapidly build datasets, train and validate machine learning models. Through this time I worked with staff biologists to design surveys, deploy equipment, and label over 12,000 frog calls in recordings.

Along with this I've worked for Quadrant Health, a medical startup, where I designed ways to use established medical panel standards like LOINK so users could take ownership of their own medical records.

// Talk about where my passions lie

I'm currently living in Guatemala learning Spanish. I've had the opportunity to talk to EFTCs School of Hope, a school for impoverished youth in Jocotenango, where staff are stretched thin. They have many students who are underdeveloped in different ways. Digital learning platforms have helped them cater lessons to individual students and fill these gaps.

Seeing DataKinds work with Riders for Health and your open source contributions gives me confidence this work is something I would be excited to do. Thank you for your consideration, I'm looking forward to hearing more about your vision for this project.

#v(1fr)

Yours sincerely, 

Carter James Andrew

#v(1fr)
